% The obligation variant of site_variant.fspy: argP *demands* Q instead of
% presuming it.  Once killR refutes R (and with it argP2), P and Q support
% only each other: an unfounded loop.  Grounded: P, Q UNDEC; preferred:
% {P, Q IN} and {P, Q OUT}.  Credulously the witness accepts P through the
% loop alone; judged by what each expression stands for (tasks.org,
% aida-judge-by-what-it-stands-for) no derivation in argP's term holds,
% and the result is the bare delegation of P, =!IN:P=.
lk.
declare P,Q,R:bool.
declare rp:(R->P).
declare pq:(P->Q).
declare qp:(Q->P).
declare nR:(~R).

argument argP : (P).
cut (Q->P) th.
axiom qp.
elim.
next.
axiom.
dixi.

argument argQ : (Q).
cut (P->Q) th.
axiom pq.
elim.
next.
axiom.
dixi.

argument argP2 : (P).
cut (R->P) th2.
axiom rp.
elim.
by default.
next.
axiom.
dixi.

counterargument killR : (R).
cut (~R) x.
axiom nR.
elim.
axiom.
dixi.

evaluate issue :P.
evaluate argQ.
evaluate argP.
